  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 247 AM CDT Sun Oct 4 2026\n\nWater vapor imagery early this morning shows a shortwave trough \nmoving from the Upper Midwest into the Great Lakes/ Southern Ontario \nregion. This shortwave will help push a weak front southward \nthrough the area this morning. This will help reinforce the \nseasonable temperatures and dry airmass over the region. High \npressure will settle over the region tonight into Monday, \nresulting continued cool overnight lows with widespread middle \n40s forecast for Monday morning. With light winds, sheltered/ \ncold air drainage areas will see cooler temperatures, in the \nlower 40s. \n\nThat high pressure will shift to over the East Coast by \nTuesday, with southerly flow returning for Tuesday. This warm \nadvection is aided by increasing 500mb heights and 1000-500mb \nthicknesses as upper ridging builds over the western US. Given \nthis, we'll see highs climb back above normal Tuesday with highs\nin the upper 70s expected. Highs return to the 80s for mid to \nlate week, which is between 10 and 15 degrees above normal by \nthe end of next week. \n\nThere is one timeframe when precipitation potential is trending \nupward. Thursday, a warm front starts to lift northward through the \narea. This may provide just enough lift during the day to get a few \nshowers going. Otherwise, the better chances arrive overnight when \nthe low-level jet kicks in and enhances lift on the nose of \nthat jet from eastern Nebraska through northeastern Missouri. \nChances still look fairly low (around 15% currently), though they\nare trending higher.",
